# MAHFAZA.CO ANDROID — MASTER DIRECTIVE V16
## Notification Center + XP Economy + Level Progression + Animated Student & Coach Experience

You are working on the existing Mahfaza.co Android application in Google AI Studio. The application uses Kotlin, Jetpack Compose, Material 3, Room, Repository, ViewModel, StateFlow, and Navigation Compose.

Implement this directive as a **single integrated upgrade to the existing codebase**. Do not create a new application, replace working screens unnecessarily, or introduce a parallel architecture.

## 1. NON-NEGOTIABLE IMPLEMENTATION RULES

1. Inspect the current source code, Room entities, DAOs, repositories, ViewModels, navigation graph, notification permissions, XP calculation, reward redemption, and existing tests before making changes.
2. Reuse existing implementations wherever possible. Update the current files and data model rather than creating duplicate screens, repositories, entities, or navigation destinations.
3. Keep the current local-first architecture. **Do not introduce Supabase, a remote backend, API keys, or fake cloud synchronization.**
4. Room is the source of truth for persistent local data. Use transactions for operations that must update multiple records atomically.
5. Preserve existing user data. Do not use destructive database migrations or wipe the Room database to make the new features work. Add proper versioned migrations.
6. Keep all existing working modules, including login, roles, student and coach dashboards, weekly programs, study submissions, exams, task approvals, messaging, rewards, XP history, profile photos, and PDF exports.
7. Do not claim that notifications, animations, builds, tests, or persistence work unless they have actually been implemented and verified.
8. All user-facing text must be in natural Turkish, with correct Turkish characters.
9. Do not leave placeholder buttons, hardcoded statistics, fake notifications, or decorative progress indicators that do not reflect real data.
10. Before finishing, run the available build and tests, fix regressions, and provide an evidence-based implementation report.

